Completed
The complete upstream delta (1957 paths) is preserved, with two additional
license files, exact hashes, executable modes, and a binary-capable patch.
The vendor snapshot is an overlay against the pinned FreeBSD base; it does
not duplicate the entire operating system. upstream/ remains byte-identical
to the pinned revision. Local changes live in the ordered patches/ series.

0001 repairs connection close/destructor ownership, reference-underflow
checks, reversed reaper eligibility tests, unterminated peer identity strings,
partial writes, EINTR handling, SIGPIPE suppression and response status
semantics. Failed requests previously could send both NAK and ACK; the
patched dispatcher sends one result. Cleanup eligibility requires job cleanup
and zero references. This is not a proof of all event-loop lifetime safety.
The old __maybe_unused macro is renamed to avoid modern sys/cdefs.h collision.

0002 validates and bounds cleanup payloads before queuing work, parses the
received payload instead of reading from forwarding recipients, gives each
cleanup job ownership of its own entries, and frees those entries on teardown.
It releases SEND_INFO references, zeroes outgoing message headers, checks ELF
payload length and location termination, bounds input frames to 64 MiB, avoids
one-past-end vector indexing for header-only requests, and removes reads of an
uninitialized error variable in socket setup. Cleanup limits are 4096 names
of at most 4096 bytes each. These are explicit experimental resource limits;
the native-size_t protocol has NOT been made architecture-independent.

Validation
Run with a NEW, empty output directory under /usr/obj:
  python3.12 contrib/hypertrace/tests/run.py /usr/obj/hypertrace-check
The driver verifies vendor hashes, applies both patches to an isolated copy,
reproduces nine failing upstream connection cases, and runs eleven passing
patched connection checks. Actual socketpair I/O is used, with linker wrappers
injecting short writes/EINTR deterministically. The only substituted daemon
services are logging functions. No daemon or device is started.
It then compiles all eleven daemon translation units using all logical CPUs,
links dtraced, and runs cleanup-parser tests with address/undefined-behavior
sanitizers. Actual SEND_INFO and CLEANUP job ownership/reference lifetimes
are also exercised with sanitized connection/job code, linked to the real
daemon dependencies. The upstream Makefile's -Wno-gcc-compat allowance is retained for
Clang cleanup attributes; other build warnings are errors.
Successful results: /usr/obj/5BSD-hypertrace-validation/qualified/
This is component qualification, not end-to-end daemon/VM qualification.

Remaining before a working 5BSD HyperTrace VM
1. Port the upstream libdtrace/DIF/ELF/type changes to the current compiler
   and retain existing providers, exporters and probe contracts. Build and
   run the upstream HyperTrace compiler tests against that port.
2. Integrate host/guest runtime hooks, tagged guest addresses, page-table
   walking and virtio transport with the current DTrace and bhyve interfaces.
   Review all guest lengths, types, addresses, quotas and ownership boundaries.
3. Implement guest reconnect/replay using explicit session generations and
   acknowledged program state. Retry must be idempotent; revoked programs
   must never replay. Test crash before/after acknowledgment, repeated reconnect,
   partial transfer, host daemon restart and simultaneous guests.
4. Integrate save/restore/reset: stop or drain tracing before snapshot, create
   a new restore generation, rebuild probe maps and clock anchors, and reject
   stale callbacks. Test snapshots with outstanding transport messages and
   actions, repeated restore, clone, teardown and a disconnected collector.
5. Qualify ordinary SDT/FBT first, then USDT and kinst explicitly. No blanket
   provider compatibility is established. Confirm guest/user memory faults
   remain contained, and exclude early boot and tracing recursion paths.
6. Finish the daemon protocol/concurrency audit: handshake framing/timeouts,
   message ordering, active-job re-enqueue after disconnect, kqueue event-batch
   pointer lifetime, allocation failure, file I/O completeness and socket-info
   snapshot races remain review areas. The existing synchronous control I/O
   can block; retrying EINTR does not solve deadlines or nonblocking framing.
7. Run matched host/guest builds in a disposable qualification environment.
   Measure VM exits, throughput, p99 application latency and trace loss under
   increasing event rates, comparing hypercalls with batched guest collection.

Nothing is wired into the default world/kernel build, installed, loaded or
started by this milestone. Neither reconnect replay nor VM save/restore is
claimed fixed. dev and the original dirty /usr/src checkout remain separate.

Probe inventories must exclude this unbuilt vendor comparison overlay; it
is not additional installed 5BSD probe coverage.
